    "authorAssociation": "NONE",
    "body": "With vim-fugitive, `:G` is available for `:Git`, how can I set up the same with `mini.git`?",
    "bodyHTML": "<p dir=\"auto\">With vim-fugitive, <code class=\"notranslate\">:G</code> is available for <code class=\"notranslate\">:Git</code>, how can I set up the same with <code class=\"notranslate\">mini.git</code>?</p>",
    "bodyText": "With vim-fugitive, :G is available for :Git, how can I set up the same with mini.git?",
    "category": {
        "createdAt": "2022-01-02T14:24:09Z",
        "description": "Chat about anything and everything here",
        "emoji": ":speech_balloon:",
        "emojiHTML": "<div>💬</div>",
        "id": "DIC_kwDOFoksYM4CAgqM",
        "isAnswerable": false,
        "name": "General",
        "slug": "general",
        "updatedAt": "2022-01-02T14:24:09Z"
    },
    "closed": true,
    "closedAt": "2024-05-31T16:41:41Z",
    "comment_count": 1,
    "comment_data": [
        {
            "author": {
                "avatarUrl": "https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/24854248?u=d09fd974c6720eba3039db6beb2f1c1efecdb51d&v=4",
                "login": "echasnovski",
                "resourcePath": "/echasnovski",
                "url": "https://github.com/echasnovski"
            },
            "authorAssociation": "MEMBER",
            "body": "As far as I know, there doesn't appear to be an easy way to create a copy of an existing user command.\r\n\r\nThe best solution seems to be to create an command line abbreviation for `G` which would expand to `Git` after pressing space.\r\n\r\nFollowing [this answer](https://stackoverflow.com/a/3879737), there are at least two approaches:\r\n\r\n- Easy: `vim.cmd('cnoreabbrev G Git')`. I think this will be enough 99% of times. However, it will expand whenever a separate `G` is entered in a command line, including during search (like after `/`) or when it is not at the first column.\r\n- More robust: `vim.cmd([[cnoreabbrev <expr> G ((getcmdtype() is# ':' && getcmdline() is# 'G')?('Git'):('G'))]])`. If you use Neovim 0.10, this can also be rewritten with `vim.keymap.set()`:\r\n\r\n    ```lua\r\n    local expand_g = function() return (vim.fn.getcmdtype() == ':' and vim.fn.getcmdline() == 'G') and 'Git' or 'G' end\r\n    vim.keymap.set('ca', 'G', expand_g, { expr = true })\r\n    ```",
            "bodyHTML": "<p dir=\"auto\">As far as I know, there doesn't appear to be an easy way to create a copy of an existing user command.</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">The best solution seems to be to create an command line abbreviation for <code class=\"notranslate\">G</code> which would expand to <code class=\"notranslate\">Git</code> after pressing space.</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Following <a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/a/3879737\" rel=\"nofollow\">this answer</a>, there are at least two approaches:</p>\n<ul dir=\"auto\">\n<li>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Easy: <code class=\"notranslate\">vim.cmd('cnoreabbrev G Git')</code>. I think this will be enough 99% of times. However, it will expand whenever a separate <code class=\"notranslate\">G</code> is entered in a command line, including during search (like after <code class=\"notranslate\">/</code>) or when it is not at the first column.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p dir=\"auto\">More robust: <code class=\"notranslate\">vim.cmd([[cnoreabbrev &lt;expr&gt; G ((getcmdtype() is# ':' &amp;&amp; getcmdline() is# 'G')?('Git'):('G'))]])</code>. If you use Neovim 0.10, this can also be rewritten with <code class=\"notranslate\">vim.keymap.set()</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"highlight highlight-source-lua notranslate position-relative overflow-auto\" dir=\"auto\" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content=\"local expand_g = function() return (vim.fn.getcmdtype() == ':' and vim.fn.getcmdline() == 'G') and 'Git' or 'G' end\nvim.keymap.set('ca', 'G', expand_g, { expr = true })\"><pre class=\"notranslate\"><span class=\"pl-k\">local</span> <span class=\"pl-en\">expand_g</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">=</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">function</span>() <span class=\"pl-k\">return</span> (<span class=\"pl-smi\">vim</span>.<span class=\"pl-e\">fn</span>.<span class=\"pl-c1\">getcmdtype</span>() <span class=\"pl-k\">==</span> <span class=\"pl-s\"><span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span>:<span class=\"pl-pds\">' </span></span><span class=\"pl-k\">and</span> <span class=\"pl-smi\">vim</span>.<span class=\"pl-e\">fn</span>.<span class=\"pl-c1\">getcmdline</span>() <span class=\"pl-k\">==</span> <span class=\"pl-s\"><span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span>G<span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span></span>) <span class=\"pl-k\">and</span> <span class=\"pl-s\"><span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span>Git<span class=\"pl-pds\">' </span></span><span class=\"pl-k\">or</span> <span class=\"pl-s\"><span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span>G<span class=\"pl-pds\">' </span></span><span class=\"pl-k\">end</span>\n<span class=\"pl-smi\">vim</span>.<span class=\"pl-e\">keymap</span>.<span class=\"pl-c1\">set</span>(<span class=\"pl-s\"><span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span>ca<span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span></span>, <span class=\"pl-s\"><span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span>G<span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span></span>, <span class=\"pl-smi\">expand_g</span>, { <span class=\"pl-smi\">expr</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">=</span> <span class=\"pl-c1\">true</span> })</pre></div>\n</li>\n</ul>",
            "bodyText": "As far as I know, there doesn't appear to be an easy way to create a copy of an existing user command.\nThe best solution seems to be to create an command line abbreviation for G which would expand to Git after pressing space.\nFollowing this answer, there are at least two approaches:\n\n\nEasy: vim.cmd('cnoreabbrev G Git'). I think this will be enough 99% of times. However, it will expand whenever a separate G is entered in a command line, including during search (like after /) or when it is not at the first column.\n\n\nMore robust: vim.cmd([[cnoreabbrev <expr> G ((getcmdtype() is# ':' && getcmdline() is# 'G')?('Git'):('G'))]]). If you use Neovim 0.10, this can also be rewritten with vim.keymap.set():\nlocal expand_g = function() return (vim.fn.getcmdtype() == ':' and vim.fn.getcmdline() == 'G') and 'Git' or 'G' end\nvim.keymap.set('ca', 'G', expand_g, { expr = true })",
